.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii sys_hostname.2
.\"
.TH SYS_HOSTNAME 2 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_hostname \- get system hostname
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/syscall.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_hostname(char *" buf ", size_t " len ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sys_hostname()
system call retrieves the system's hostname and stores it in the buffer
pointed to by
.IR buf .
At most
.I len
bytes are written to the buffer, including the terminating null byte.
.PP
If the hostname is longer than
.IR len \-1
characters, it is truncated to fit, and the buffer is always null-terminated.
.PP
The hostname is set during kernel initialization and identifies the machine
on the network.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, returns 0.
.PP
On error, returns \-1 (with errno set appropriately, though this implementation
does not currently set errno).
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I buf
points to an invalid address.
.SH NOTES
This is a Substrate-specific system call.
The portable method to retrieve the hostname is via
.BR gethostname (3),
which may be implemented using this system call.
.PP
The
.BR uname (2)
system call also returns the hostname in its
.I nodename
field.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <sys/syscall.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char hostname[256];
    if (sys_hostname(hostname, sizeof(hostname)) == 0) {
        printf("Hostname: %s\en", hostname);
    }
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gethostname (3),
.BR sethostname (2),
.BR uname (2),
.BR intro (2)
